#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555943} :activity/id #uuid "efce7d92-bdf5-5126-8ee2-95a76a4e27f8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050612 :topic/id #uuid "58cd99c7-ea79-5204-8fe5-0ad9cfae548a" :topic/math-academy-id 918} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555875 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cd36490-edd9-5214-a621-f23cfa7c2994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(11,33)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555022} {:answer/id #uuid "5962f9df-804d-53d5-9166-dfb18748621f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(11,9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555196} {:answer/id #uuid "670e1e26-754f-56e5-843b-304f893b1022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(7,33)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555234} {:answer/id #uuid "70d55769-d43e-5f7b-bd36-e8679f82da3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(5,15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555257} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1fdf93-08d1-5f0d-a2b7-865f4471e783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(5,9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555493}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555022} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f442e1a-b907-5d38-960a-0590874e2f6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e1d8b86d-ac0b-5d14-99b3-68fba3ea8058" :question/math-academy-id "q-159749" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y=-3{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[-3{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =-3\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+2\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {\\mu }_{1}+2\\cdot {\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =3+8 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=5,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=3,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[-3{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =(-3)^{2}\\cdot 5+{2}^{2}\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =45+12-24 \\\\  & =33.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(11,33).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "01c244ab-6c29-59ab-8921-3e9ab03051b2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555941}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555889 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555802 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37c00ba7-4e07-54ad-bdbc-1156749cc2e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8641" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555120} {:answer/id #uuid "5189a220-76ea-5091-b49a-16f052b2f1d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8138" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555182} {:answer/id #uuid "52750003-2d76-57fb-a067-cb80127c33cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8413" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555187} {:answer/id #uuid "82993aab-8bd6-5d61-9861-50ee8d1879b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8587" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555301} {:answer/id #uuid "e4152bbf-4ef2-5a5d-a6e2-4af52f0157e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8359" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555559}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555187} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e5cc2f0-6725-5001-8935-9b10d946cfa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4fcf6802-fc48-589d-8b96-17389df12736" :question/math-academy-id "q-159804" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y=2{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-{X}_{3}.$ Find $P(Y\\le -2).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y=2{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2-1-4 \\\\  & =-7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =14+4+7 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-7,25).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(Y\\le -2).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le -2) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{-2-(-7)}{\\sqrt{25}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\le 1) & =Φ(1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(1)=0.8413.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\le 1)=0.8413.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le -2)=0.8413.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "30857c75-58d2-5428-a216-a30804c4b0dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555923}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555923 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555805 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11f333d9-733e-5389-8cda-5e1dfd790b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8289" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555035} {:answer/id #uuid "2270f878-d33b-5b4b-94a5-97c69457888a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9713" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555068} {:answer/id #uuid "4c5cc44d-5d18-5731-a82e-007a6010f363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8611" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555170} {:answer/id #uuid "6b48f9bd-b48b-5648-9060-b92aee8c6760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8002" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555245} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6bab9b-a8ad-52b9-8e04-2ce38c845042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9424" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555579}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555245} :answer-field/id #uuid "5147a116-059b-5bc9-9e03-f4ccc1268a8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "54165c1c-b41c-51d6-bf2e-f7ee2418543e" :question/math-academy-id "q-159922" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y=2{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+2{X}_{3}.$ Find $P(-6<Y\\le 12).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7673\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7704\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7734\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7967\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7995\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8023\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.9\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8238\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8264\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8289\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9554\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9564\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9573\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9641\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9649\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9656\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.9\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9713\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9719\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9726\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y=2{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+2{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2+0+2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =14+6+20 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nY∼N(0,40).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(-6<Y\\le 12).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-6<Y\\le 12) & =P(\\frac{-6-0}{\\sqrt{40}}<Z\\le \\frac{12-0}{\\sqrt{40}}) \\\\  & =P(-0.95<Z\\le 1.90)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.95<Z\\le 1.90) & =P(Z\\le 1.90)-P(Z\\le -0.95) \\\\  & =Φ(1.90)-Φ(-0.95).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.95) & =0.8289 \\\\ Φ(1.90) & =0.9713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the symmetry of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.95)=1-Φ(0.95)=1-0.8289=0.1711.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.95<Z\\le 1.90)=0.9713-0.1711=0.8002.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(-6<Y\\le 12)=0.8002.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "99861a14-37fb-5d5b-8d38-23e43e22fe20"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555941 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555784 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54c9603b-92f1-56f6-99ca-2927d578549b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(-1,18)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555190} {:answer/id #uuid "6352137f-a35d-5422-a665-50d3360c88ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(-9,18)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555220} {:answer/id #uuid "a88422ce-eaac-5228-b582-ab5651637d03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(-9,27)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555404} {:answer/id #uuid "ea29a78b-d5bd-58c5-bf5a-061d39d47053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(-1,27)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555575} {:answer/id #uuid "fccad168-961f-5565-88d9-b5040f528958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(-1,3\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555629}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555575} :answer-field/id #uuid "811b92f6-defd-55cc-8044-3d28a01fece4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3261919b-dda9-5522-ac76-67204c4f5250" :question/math-academy-id "q-159750" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y=-5{X}_{1}-4{X}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=1$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[-5{X}_{1}-4{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =-5\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]-4\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =-5\\cdot {\\mu }_{1}-4\\cdot {\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-5+4 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=3,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=2,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[-5{X}_{1}-4{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =(-5)^{2}\\cdot 3+(-4)^{2}\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-5)\\cdot (-4)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =75+32-80 \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-1,27).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d04d0fda-bcd1-5335-8424-d33cc219d276" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555889}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555943 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555758 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2846048f-2417-521c-9d91-127a15b07b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(-1,\\sqrt{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555085} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd86d90-af5c-5230-b370-aa075ec9497c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(-7,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555095} {:answer/id #uuid "370d24aa-0151-57e8-bd1a-dbbffce961f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(3,14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555116} {:answer/id #uuid "3d47ed6b-2a88-5d85-914f-29d9c5213390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(3,\\sqrt{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555132} {:answer/id #uuid "daee6f33-0dfe-59c6-8639-bc8f66d75e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(-7,14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555530}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555530} :answer-field/id #uuid "70fb0548-9e58-5eec-98d2-d882b0569a5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0076dfd1-19a9-575a-953a-f75321364331" :question/math-academy-id "q-159748" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=-5$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]-\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =-5-2 \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=3,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=7,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 3+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 7+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =3+7+4 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-7,14).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d6927984-d02e-577e-8182-e0ef43721475" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555875}}] :activity/title "The Bivariate Normal Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555002}]}
